A||||7||Fformula88||Why does buying stuff turn into a hassle?||||8||
Z||000000||Fformula88||11-29-2001||12:16 PM||||Here is the story.  I needed to get some snow tires for my daily driver (Ford Probe) and as usual, went to Dunn Tire.  Its a local chain that has incredible customer service, and even replaced my tires free once under the road hazard warrenty when someone took an ice pick to the sidewals!<P>The location near my house didn't have the correct size, but a location about 15 miles off did.  They offered to ship them in but since it was Saturday I told him I'd just go to the other location.  They called ahead so I wouldn't have to wait when I got there.  Everything is good so far!<P>So when I get there the guy meets me at the door!  I am starting to get really impressed.  I tell him what tire I wanted to make sure nothing got mixed up and that I wanted them studded.  He gave me a slightly funny look, said ok, and took the car in.  Everything is ok so far.<P>They finish, I take the car home (gee studs are noisy!) and decide to look it all over.  First... the car is covered in aluminum dust apparently from them cleaning some old rims in the garage for another customer.  This upset me a bit since I had washed it the day before!<P>Then I looked at the so-called studded tires.  The middle treads apparently were scared of the stud gun or something because all of those studs were MIA  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  The outer studs were there, some were half out, others were at 45 degree angles to the tread blocks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  Now I am very upset!<P>So I called them on Monday and they said their stud gun wasn't working right and the studs probably "fell out!".  Um... why not tell me that while I was there?  I think they were just lying to me because either they have no idea how to stud a tire or were in a hurry and did a crappy job.<P>So I take them back to the original location and tell them I want to return them.  They were much more gracious, and may have saved a customer!  They got a complete set of new tires and mounted them for me!  They also offered to stud them, but I took them without the studs.  I was still a little fearful of the broken stud gun even though it was a seperate location.<P>What morons that other place must have had.  Now I just have to decide whether I want to stud them or not!  Sorry, I just had to vent a little!<P>PS, for any local guys, the bad location is Hertel in Buffalo... good is Walden in Lancaster!<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/formulasig.JPG">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||8
Z||000001||btoth||11-29-2001||12:55 PM||||It's the simple things that always turn out to be a pain.<P>I went to get my oil changed at the local Valvoline and specifically said, just change the oil and the filter, don't touch anything else.  (I knew the guy that was in charge so he actually listened to me.)<P>Well, they still attemped to change the air filter... which I let go because it was really funny to watch.  Of course, then they found out that it was a K&N and had to put the cover back on.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  If they had read the K&N sticker, it would've saved them some time.<P>Then, they were done with the oil... very nice new clean oil in my new engine, I was happy.  But, the morons inflated the back tires to 45psi!!!  But left the front ones alone.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  kind of an awkward drive to the nearest parking lot to deflate my tires.<P>Sigh.  And to think, I was trying to make their job simpler by only having them do the one thing.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Master_Sushi||11-29-2001||01:19 PM||||I got through last winter in my 88 coupe with unstuded snow tires. Never got stuck and had lots of fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. Last winter wasn't that harsh though. Buffalo gets way more snow then the falls.<P><BR>Good luck.||||reg||6
Z||000003||MinnGreenGT||11-29-2001||01:51 PM||||Didn't realize that studded tires were allowed anywhere besides the mountains and Alaska! We can't even use chains or anything here (aside from getting un-stuck).  Oh well, I've learned to "ice-skate" my vehicles so I'm pretty used to it now!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fformula88||11-29-2001||02:40 PM||||They do have restrictions on studs here... not sure the exact dates but I believe you can run them between Nov 1 and Apr 1.  <P>I'll say its the simple things!  I left a part out!  These snows were to be mounted on 14 inch steel rims.  I have an extra set of rims, and the wheels that are on the car are 15" aluminum rims.  After my disgust with the studs I put the aluminum rims back on while I dealt with the shop.  The car road awfully funny on them, like one was flat.  So when I checked the pressures... I noticed 3 rims had brand new valve stems!  The must have broke the seals on three of the four rims before realizing 14 inch tires just do not go on 15 inch rims!  They then had the following inflations for the tires:  28 on the one they did not touch, 34, 37 and 38 on the ones they did!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P>Sushi, I made it thru last winter too, but not well!!  I did a 180 on dick road after making a turn at only 5-10 MPH!  I almost pulled the same menuever on the 33 a couple of times.  The car simply had no traction.  Sicne the studs are more for ice, I thought they would be helpful but I may just stick with the snow tires without them.  They are kinda loud, and almost my whole commute is on the thruway which is the best plowed and salted road around!  Yep, the Falls does not get it like Buffalo.  I live in Depew near the airport, and we got more snow here than the city gets!  Darn lake effect!||||reg||1
Z||000005||AkursedX||11-29-2001||09:09 PM||||I have some studded rear tires on my Fiero, and unstudded winter tires on the front of it.  This is my first winter with a rear wheel drive car, so I'm sure it'll be interesting to say the least.<P>Last winter my car never got stuck.  It was a '94 Plymouth Duster and it had the same snow tires on it that my Fiero has.  I have never owned a better winter car.  <P>Hopefully we will get a nice winter this year to make up for last year's debacle.<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fformula88||11-30-2001||10:18 AM||||I hope so too Akursed!  It would be nice to have an easy winter!  So far so good too!  This November is the first month ever where we haven't gotten any snow!  WOOHOO!!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Master_Sushi||11-30-2001||02:03 PM||||I had a few uncontroled skids last winter. The scariest thing that happend was on the I90. I was driving home from work, and it was snowing all day. Must have been two or three inches on the roads. I lost stearing many times on the highway, because the road was all slush and ice. I still don't know how I kept the car in control and out of the ditch. It was funny because I was passing 4X4's and suv's that span into the ditch. <P><BR>This year I'm puting a few bags of sand in the front of my fiero. I will be driving on 16 inch all season pep boys tires so I need all the help I can get. I dont know what I was thinking when I sold my tech rims with snow tires with my 88 coupe. Oh well, I'm in for some fun this winter.||||reg||1
